{
 "format": "mechanism-workshop",
 "version": 1,
 "id": "example-gears",
 "name": "Two-stage gear reduction",
 "description": "A two-stage reduction: 16-tooth pinion → 48-tooth gear, sharing a lay shaft with a 14-tooth pinion → 56-tooth output gear (module 2). Overall ratio (48/16)·(56/14) = 12 : 1. A constant 0.3 N·m torque loads the output drum.\n\nChange any tooth count: the involute geometry is regenerated, the shafts (and their holes in the plate) move to the new centre distance m·(z₁+z₂)/2, the teeth are re-phased, and the speed ratio and motor torque in the plots follow.\n\nGear meshes are IDEAL rolling couplings at the pitch point (no tooth-by-tooth contact, backlash dynamics or friction losses). The drawn teeth are true involutes phased consistently with that constraint; the interference checker verifies they do not overlap.\n\nThe motor is torque-limited (50 N·mm) and the load behaves like a weight on the drum: when the ratio or the load asks for more than the limit, the diagnostics report the motor saturating and being driven backwards.",
 "createdAt": "2026-01-01T00:00:00.000Z",
 "modifiedAt": "2026-01-01T00:00:00.000Z",
